# GTK3 port manifest — one line per package, in DEPENDENCY ORDER.
#
#   <section>  <name>  <version>  [explicit-URL]  [gitlab-repo]
#
# Same format as ../packages.list and read by the same scripts; see ../build.sh
# for why the two ports share one driver.  Section "-" means the package is not
# an X.Org one, which is all of them here, so every line carries an explicit
# download URL with %VERSION% expanded from the version column.
#
# The NAME must match the directory the tarball unpacks into, because the build
# driver finds a package's tree by that name.  Two are not what they look like:
# GNOME publishes GTK 3.24 as "gtk", not "gtk+", and the DejaVu release is
# "dejavu-fonts-ttf-<v>".
#
# Build order matters and is not alphabetical.  Do not sort this file.
#
# NOT here, because the X.Org port already builds them into the same sysroot:
# zlib (imported by ../import-base-libs.sh), libpng, jpeg, pixman, freetype,
# fontconfig and expat.  Adding them here would put two versions of each in one
# sysroot.  Their versions were bumped in ../packages.list for this port.

# ---- C++ runtime --------------------------------------------------------
# HarfBuzz and Enchant are C++, and nothing in this tree was C++ before them.
# Only libstdc++-v3 is built out of this tree, with the host compiler and the
# port's wrappers; the rest of GCC is not touched.  The version tracks the
# build host's g++ (13.3.0), because that compiler's ABI is what the resulting
# library has to match.
-         gcc                13.3.0   https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/gcc/gcc-%VERSION%/gcc-%VERSION%.tar.xz

# ---- ICU ----------------------------------------------------------------
# The Unicode and localisation library, for WebKit -- which requires it
# outright (find_package(ICU REQUIRED)) and also requires HarfBuzz's ICU
# integration, which is why this sits ABOVE harfbuzz in the order.  Built
# twice from one tree like GLib: the cross build packages its data library by
# RUNNING the tools (genrb, pkgdata), so a native copy of them is built first
# and pointed at with --with-cross-build.  The tarball is the icu4c source
# release; its top-level directory is plain "icu", which unpack.sh renames.
-         icu                78.3     https://github.com/unicode-org/icu/releases/download/release-78.3/icu4c-78.3-sources.tgz

# ---- GLib and its dependencies ------------------------------------------
# libffi is how GObject calls a closure whose signature is only known at run
# time (signals, bindings); PCRE2 is GRegex.
-         libffi             3.7.1    https://github.com/libffi/libffi/releases/download/v%VERSION%/libffi-%VERSION%.tar.gz
-         pcre2              10.47    https://github.com/PCRE2Project/pcre2/releases/download/pcre2-%VERSION%/pcre2-%VERSION%.tar.gz
# The libc's own iconv covers UTF-8, ASCII, Latin-1 and Latin-9 -- enough for
# the X core fonts, nowhere near enough for a mail client, which meets whatever
# charset a message declares.  GNU libiconv renames its entry points to
# libiconv_open/libiconv/libiconv_close in its header, so the two
# implementations coexist without either shadowing the other.
-         libiconv           1.19     https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/libiconv/libiconv-%VERSION%.tar.gz
# Only gettext-runtime is built (see make_dirs in ../build.sh): that is libintl,
# which every package below links for its translations.  gettext-tools is a
# build-host concern -- the host's own msgfmt compiles the .po files.
-         gettext            0.26     https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/gettext/gettext-%VERSION%.tar.xz
-         glib               2.88.3   https://download.gnome.org/sources/glib/2.88/glib-%VERSION%.tar.xz

# ---- image loading ------------------------------------------------------
# libpng and jpeg come from the X.Org port; TIFF is the one gdk-pixbuf format
# nothing else needed yet.
-         tiff               4.7.2    https://download.osgeo.org/libtiff/tiff-%VERSION%.tar.xz

# ---- text shaping and rendering -----------------------------------------
# FriBidi resolves the bidirectional algorithm, HarfBuzz maps a string plus a
# font to positioned glyphs, Cairo rasterises, Pango puts the three together.
# HarfBuzz is where the C++ runtime above is first used in anger.
-         fribidi            1.0.16   https://github.com/fribidi/fribidi/releases/download/v%VERSION%/fribidi-%VERSION%.tar.xz
-         harfbuzz           14.3.0   https://github.com/harfbuzz/harfbuzz/releases/download/%VERSION%/harfbuzz-%VERSION%.tar.xz
-         cairo              1.18.4   https://cairographics.org/releases/cairo-%VERSION%.tar.xz
-         pango              1.58.0   https://download.gnome.org/sources/pango/1.58/pango-%VERSION%.tar.xz

# ---- the rest of GTK's foundation ---------------------------------------
-         gdk-pixbuf         2.44.7   https://download.gnome.org/sources/gdk-pixbuf/2.44/gdk-pixbuf-%VERSION%.tar.xz
# ATK is the accessibility interface GTK3 implements.  2.38.0 is its final
# release; there will not be another.
-         atk                2.38.0   https://download.gnome.org/sources/atk/2.38/atk-%VERSION%.tar.xz
# libepoxy dispatches OpenGL calls.  GTK3 requires it whether or not there is a
# GL implementation to dispatch to, and here there is not -- see meson_opts.
# No release tarball is published for 1.5.10, so this is the tag export.
-         libepoxy           1.5.10   https://github.com/anholt/libepoxy/archive/refs/tags/%VERSION%.tar.gz

# ---- the toolkit --------------------------------------------------------
# GNOME publishes the 3.24 series under the module name "gtk", not "gtk+".
-         gtk                3.24.52  https://download.gnome.org/sources/gtk/3.24/gtk-%VERSION%.tar.xz

# ---- fonts and icons ----------------------------------------------------
# Not optional and not decoration.  The image ships bitmap PCF fonts only, and
# Pango renders through FreeType: with no scalable font installed every GTK
# program comes up with blank or boxed text and no error anywhere.
#
# hicolor is the fallback theme GTK's icon lookup requires by specification.
# Adwaita is pinned to 3.38.0 deliberately -- it is the last release whose icons
# are PNG.  Every later one is symbolic SVG, which would mean librsvg, which
# would mean a Rust toolchain.
-         dejavu-fonts-ttf   2.37     https://github.com/dejavu-fonts/dejavu-fonts/releases/download/version_2_37/dejavu-fonts-ttf-%VERSION%.tar.bz2

# Liberation is not a second opinion about how text should look -- it is the
# METRIC-COMPATIBLE substitute for Arial, Times New Roman and Courier New, and
# fontconfig's 30-metric-aliases.conf maps those three names onto it the moment
# it is installed.  Without it every page whose CSS says `Arial, Helvetica,
# sans-serif' -- which is most of the web that does not ship its own webfont --
# was laid out in DejaVu Sans, whose glyphs are appreciably wider.  Text then
# takes more room than the page was designed for: navigation bars overflow,
# containers that clip do so in the wrong place, and elements land on top of
# links, which is why they could not be clicked on debian.org and freebsd.org
# while sites carrying their own webfonts (cnn.com, spiegel.de) were fine.
#
# 2.00.1 rather than the current 2.1.5 because it is the last release published
# as ready-made TTFs: upstream now ships only sources, which are built with
# fontforge.  The metrics -- the entire point of this font -- are the same.
-         liberation-fonts-ttf 2.00.1 https://releases.pagure.org/liberation-fonts/liberation-fonts-ttf-%VERSION%.tar.gz

# Noto Sans covers what DejaVu and Liberation do not, and is asked for by name
# by a growing number of sites.  This is the Latin/Greek/Cyrillic release only
# -- the full Noto set is hundreds of megabytes of scripts nothing here renders.
-         NotoSans           v2.015   https://github.com/notofonts/latin-greek-cyrillic/releases/download/NotoSans-%VERSION%/NotoSans-%VERSION%.zip
-         hicolor-icon-theme 0.18     https://icon-theme.freedesktop.org/releases/hicolor-icon-theme-%VERSION%.tar.xz
-         adwaita-icon-theme 3.38.0   https://download.gnome.org/sources/adwaita-icon-theme/3.38/adwaita-icon-theme-%VERSION%.tar.xz

# ---- Claws Mail's dependencies ------------------------------------------
# startup-notification is how an application tells the window manager it is
# starting; ctwm shows the busy cursor for it.
-         startup-notification 0.12   https://www.freedesktop.org/software/startup-notification/releases/startup-notification-%VERSION%.tar.gz
# The TLS chain.  OpenSSL is already ported and libetpan uses it, but Claws
# Mail's own transport code speaks to GnuTLS and nothing else, so both are
# built: GMP for the bignums, nettle for the ciphers, libtasn1 for the
# certificate parsing.  GnuTLS is also what glib-networking is configured
# against (see build.sh), so it is the TLS behind every https:// request
# WebKit and libsoup make as well -- not Claws Mail's alone.
-         gmp                6.3.0    https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/gmp/gmp-%VERSION%.tar.xz
# MPFR sits here because it needs GMP and because gdb needs IT: gdb 17
# requires MPFR outright (there is a --with-mpfr but no --without-mpfr) to do
# target floating-point arithmetic at the debuggee's precision rather than the
# host's.  Nothing in the GTK3 stack uses it; it is here because this is the
# package set that populates the sysroot gdb links against.
-         mpfr               4.2.1    https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/mpfr/mpfr-%VERSION%.tar.xz
-         nettle             3.10.2   https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/nettle/nettle-%VERSION%.tar.gz
-         libtasn1           4.21.0   https://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/libtasn1/libtasn1-%VERSION%.tar.gz
-         gnutls             3.8.13   https://www.gnupg.org/ftp/gcrypt/gnutls/v3.8/gnutls-%VERSION%.tar.xz
# Enchant is a spell-checking abstraction and needs a backend behind it, or
# every attempt to compose a message opens an error box: Claws asks it for an
# en_US speller at that moment and takes a refusal as a failure, not as a
# feature that is switched off.  hunspell is that backend, and the SCOWL en_US
# dictionary is what hunspell reads -- a checker with no dictionary fails in
# exactly the same way as no checker at all.
#
# hunspell is C++, which the libstdc++ built at the head of this list supports.
# The dictionary is a zip, the only one in either manifest; unpack.sh grew a
# case for it because SCOWL publishes nothing else.  Its name carries the
# upstream spelling (en_US, not en-us) so the archive matches the package.
-         hunspell           1.7.2    https://github.com/hunspell/hunspell/releases/download/v%VERSION%/hunspell-%VERSION%.tar.gz
-         hunspell-en_US     2020.12.07 https://downloads.sourceforge.net/wordlist/hunspell-en_US-%VERSION%.zip
-         enchant            2.8.19   https://github.com/rrthomas/enchant/releases/download/v%VERSION%/enchant-%VERSION%.tar.gz
# libetpan implements IMAP, POP3, SMTP and NNTP.  A tag export, not a release
# tarball: it has no configure, so likeos-autogen.sh runs autogen.sh first.
-         libetpan           1.10.1   https://github.com/dinhvh/libetpan/archive/refs/tags/%VERSION%.tar.gz

# ---- the applications ---------------------------------------------------
# Claws Mail moved BELOW WebKitGTK: its fancy plugin (the HTML viewer) links
# webkit2gtk-4.1, and configure --enable-fancy-plugin fails hard if the
# library is not in the sysroot yet.  See the end of this manifest.

# ---- Mousepad and what it edits with ------------------------------------
# libxml2 is here rather than in the X manifest because nothing below GTK
# wanted it: fontconfig parses its configuration with expat.  GtkSourceView
# reads its syntax definitions and style schemes with libxml2's reader, so it
# is the editor's dependency, not the font stack's.
-         libxml2            2.15.3   https://download.gnome.org/sources/libxml2/2.15/libxml2-%VERSION%.tar.xz
# The source-editing widget: syntax highlighting, bracket matching, line
# numbers, undo -- Mousepad is a window and a menu bar around it.
#
# 4.8.4 is the newest release this can use, and newer is not better here: the
# 5.x series is GTK4-only, and the toolkit below is GTK3.  4.8.4 is the last of
# the 4.x line.
-         gtksourceview      4.8.4    https://download.gnome.org/sources/gtksourceview/4.8/gtksourceview-%VERSION%.tar.xz
-         mousepad           0.7.0    https://archive.xfce.org/src/apps/mousepad/0.7/mousepad-%VERSION%.tar.xz

# ---- PCManFM and what a file manager needs to know ----------------------
# A BUILD-host tool, like nsgenbind in the X manifest, and installed into
# .hosttools rather than the sysroot.  The LXDE packages below merge
# translations into their .desktop, .directory and .menu files with intltool,
# and their configure REFUSES to continue without it -- it is not a
# translation-only concern, since those merged files are what the desktop
# entries themselves are made from.  Built here rather than required of the
# build host: it is three Perl scripts and a configure, and asking every
# machine that builds this tree to install a package first is a worse trade.
-         intltool           0.51.0   https://deb.debian.org/debian/pool/main/i/intltool/intltool_%VERSION%.orig.tar.gz
# What a file IS, in the only form GLib can answer the question from.
# g_content_type_guess() -- which is how libfm decides a file's icon, its
# description and what opens it -- reads the shared MIME database and has no
# built-in table to fall back on.  Without this every file on the system is
# application/octet-stream: one generic icon everywhere and no default
# application for anything.  A git export, because the project publishes no
# release tarballs; nothing in it is compiled here (see install_data).
-         shared-mime-info   2.5.1    https://gitlab.freedesktop.org/xdg/shared-mime-info/-/archive/%VERSION%/shared-mime-info-%VERSION%.tar.gz
# The application menu itself: the .menu file that says which categories exist
# and in what order, and the directory entries that name them.  menu-cache
# reads it; without one, the "Open With" dialog's application list is empty
# even though the .desktop files are all present.
-         lxmenu-data        0.1.7    https://github.com/lxde/lxmenu-data/archive/refs/tags/%VERSION%.tar.gz
# libfm and menu-cache each need part of the other, so libfm is built twice
# from two copies of one tarball rather than once in place:
#
#   libfm-extra   the XML parser and string helpers, and nothing else
#                 (--with-extra-only).  menu-cache links it.
#   menu-cache    the menu reader.
#   libfm         the whole file-management library, which requires
#                 menu-cache to be installed before it will configure.
#
# Two trees rather than two passes over one, because each build begins by
# distcleaning the tree it is in: a second pass would erase the first one's
# configuration while its stamp still said it was built.
#
# All four are tag exports rather than release tarballs -- the current LXDE
# releases are published as tags and nothing else, which is why the last
# tarball on SourceForge is several versions behind.  They therefore have no
# configure script, and likeos-autogen.sh runs their autogen.sh first, exactly
# as it does for libetpan above.
-         libfm-extra        1.4.1    https://github.com/lxde/libfm/archive/refs/tags/%VERSION%.tar.gz
-         menu-cache         1.1.1    https://github.com/lxde/menu-cache/archive/refs/tags/%VERSION%.tar.gz
-         libfm              1.4.1    https://github.com/lxde/libfm/archive/refs/tags/%VERSION%.tar.gz
-         pcmanfm            1.4.0    https://github.com/lxde/pcmanfm/archive/refs/tags/%VERSION%.tar.gz

# ---- Galculator ---------------------------------------------------------
# A GTK3 desktop calculator: basic, scientific and paper modes, with the
# expression parser its scientific mode needs.  A tag export -- upstream's last
# release tarball predates the move to GitHub -- so it has no configure and
# likeos-autogen.sh makes one, as it does for the LXDE packages above.
#
# The scientific mode's input is a scanner generated by flex from
# src/flex_parser.l.  flex runs on the BUILD host and emits C, so it is a
# build-host requirement rather than a port; the same is true of the intltool
# above it.
-         galculator         2.1.4    https://github.com/galculator/galculator/archive/refs/tags/v%VERSION%.tar.gz

# ---- HexChat and the toolkit it is written against ----------------------
# HexChat's stable line is a GTK+ 2 program and has been for its whole life:
# 2.16.2 asks for gtk+-2.0 >= 2.24.0 and there is no GTK3 release to use
# instead (the gtk3 branch upstream never shipped).  So GTK 2 is built here
# beside GTK 3 rather than the program being held back or forced onto a
# toolkit it was not written for.
#
# The two coexist without any special arrangement: different sonames
# (libgtk-x11-2.0 against libgtk-3), different header directories
# (gtk-2.0/, gtk-3.0/) and different pkg-config names.  Everything BELOW the
# toolkit -- GLib, Pango, Cairo, ATK, gdk-pixbuf -- is shared, which is most of
# the stack and all of the large parts.
#
# 2.24.33 is the end of the line: GTK 2 is finished, and this is its last
# release.
-         gtk+               2.24.33  https://download.gnome.org/sources/gtk%2B/2.24/gtk%2B-%VERSION%.tar.xz
# The IRC client.  OpenSSL is already in the sysroot (the X.Org port imports
# it), which is what its TLS support links.
-         hexchat            2.16.2   https://github.com/hexchat/hexchat/releases/download/v%VERSION%/hexchat-%VERSION%.tar.xz

# ---- xfce4-terminal, and the terminal widget under it -------------------
# The stack below xfce4-terminal is deeper than the program: VTE is the
# terminal emulator itself -- the parser, the screen model, the selection, the
# scrollback -- and the Xfce libraries are the settings system its preferences
# dialog is built on.
#
# VTE's own three dependencies, none of which anything here needed before:
#   lz4      compresses the scrollback in memory.  A terminal keeps tens of
#            thousands of lines and VTE does not store them as text.
#   fmt      the C++ formatting library, used throughout VTE's own code.
#   simdutf  UTF-8 validation and transcoding.  VTE validates every byte that
#            arrives from the child, and this is what it validates with.
# All three are required, not optional: VTE has no fallback path for any of
# them.  fmt and simdutf are C++ and build with CMake; lz4 with make.
-         lz4                1.10.0   https://github.com/lz4/lz4/archive/refs/tags/v%VERSION%.tar.gz
-         fmt                12.2.0   https://github.com/fmtlib/fmt/archive/refs/tags/%VERSION%.tar.gz
-         simdutf            9.1.0    https://github.com/simdutf/simdutf/archive/refs/tags/v%VERSION%.tar.gz
# The terminal widget.  GTK3 only: the GTK4 widget is built from the same
# tree by default and there is no GTK4 here.
-         vte                0.84.1   https://download.gnome.org/sources/vte/0.84/vte-%VERSION%.tar.xz

# The Xfce libraries.  Only the three xfce4-terminal links, not a desktop:
#   libxfce4util  string, file and kiosk helpers.  No GUI.
#   xfconf        the settings store: a daemon holding a tree of typed
#                 properties and a client library that talks to it over
#                 D-Bus.  This is where every preference the terminal has
#                 is kept.
#   libxfce4ui    the shared widgets -- the about dialog, the shortcuts
#                 editor, the titled dialog -- plus libxfce4kbd-private,
#                 which is what binds the terminal's accelerators.
#
# 4.20 is the stable series; 4.21 is the development one that becomes 4.22.
-         libxfce4util       4.20.1   https://archive.xfce.org/src/xfce/libxfce4util/4.20/libxfce4util-%VERSION%.tar.bz2
-         xfconf             4.20.0   https://archive.xfce.org/src/xfce/xfconf/4.20/xfconf-%VERSION%.tar.bz2
-         libxfce4ui         4.20.2   https://archive.xfce.org/src/xfce/libxfce4ui/4.20/libxfce4ui-%VERSION%.tar.bz2
-         xfce4-terminal     1.2.0    https://archive.xfce.org/src/apps/xfce4-terminal/1.2/xfce4-terminal-%VERSION%.tar.xz
# The session bus, which is what xfconf is reached over.
#
# xfconf is a daemon holding the settings and a client library talking to it,
# and the two find each other through D-Bus -- xfconfd is started on demand
# from org.xfce.Xfconf.service rather than by anything on the image.  With no
# bus running, xfce4-terminal warns once and falls back to compiled-in
# defaults: it works, and nothing it is told in its preferences dialog
# survives being closed.
#
# GDBus in GLib is the client side and is already built; this is the daemon and
# the tools.  Its only dependency is expat, which the X.Org port already
# provides for fontconfig.
-         dbus               1.16.2   https://dbus.freedesktop.org/releases/dbus/dbus-%VERSION%.tar.xz

# ---- the WebKit platform ------------------------------------------------
# Everything below exists for one consumer each unless said otherwise:
# WebKitGTK is the engine, luakit the browser driving it, and Claws Mail's
# fancy plugin renders HTML mail with it.
#
# sqlite: WebKit's storage (localStorage, IndexedDB, the HTTP cache index)
# and luakit's bookmarks and history.  The autoconf amalgamation tarball.
-         sqlite-autoconf    3530400  https://sqlite.org/2026/sqlite-autoconf-%VERSION%.tar.gz
# brotli and woff2: downloadable-font decompression.  WOFF2 is the format
# webfonts actually arrive in; brotli is the compression under it.  Both are
# small CMake libraries from the same stable (tag exports; neither project
# publishes release tarballs).
-         brotli             1.1.0    https://github.com/google/brotli/archive/refs/tags/v%VERSION%.tar.gz
-         woff2              1.0.2    https://github.com/google/woff2/archive/refs/tags/v%VERSION%.tar.gz
# libwebp: the WebP image decoder, a required WebKit dependency (with its
# demux component, which is what WebKit actually asks pkg-config for).
-         libwebp            1.6.0    https://storage.googleapis.com/downloads.webmproject.org/releases/webp/libwebp-%VERSION%.tar.gz
# libgcrypt (over libgpg-error): the WebCrypto implementation the GTK port
# requires (USE_GCRYPT is not optional there).  libtasn1, its companion for
# certificate handling, is already above for GnuTLS.
-         libgpg-error       1.61     https://www.gnupg.org/ftp/gcrypt/libgpg-error/libgpg-error-%VERSION%.tar.bz2
-         libgcrypt          1.12.2   https://www.gnupg.org/ftp/gcrypt/libgcrypt/libgcrypt-%VERSION%.tar.bz2
# libpsl: the public-suffix list, which is how libsoup decides where a cookie
# may be scoped.  IDNA through the ICU built above.
-         libpsl             0.23.3   https://github.com/rockdaboot/libpsl/releases/download/%VERSION%/libpsl-%VERSION%.tar.gz
# nghttp2: HTTP/2 framing for libsoup.  Only the C library is built
# (--enable-lib-only); the bundled tools are C++23 programs nothing ships.
-         nghttp2            1.70.0   https://github.com/nghttp2/nghttp2/releases/download/v%VERSION%/nghttp2-%VERSION%.tar.xz
# libxslt: WebKit's XSLTProcessor, over the libxml2 already built above.
-         libxslt            1.1.45   https://download.gnome.org/sources/libxslt/1.1/libxslt-%VERSION%.tar.xz
# libsoup 3: the HTTP stack of the webkit2gtk-4.1 API line.
-         libsoup            3.6.6    https://download.gnome.org/sources/libsoup/3.6/libsoup-%VERSION%.tar.xz
# glib-networking: the GIO TLS module (GnuTLS backend).  Without it libsoup
# can speak only plain http:// -- every https:// connection fails with
# "TLS support is not available".  Purely a runtime module, dlopen'd from
# /usr/lib/gio/modules.
-         glib-networking    2.80.1   https://download.gnome.org/sources/glib-networking/2.80/glib-networking-%VERSION%.tar.xz
# ---- XF86VidMode, for GLX -----------------------------------------------
# libGL's GLX code uses the VidMode extension for gamma queries; the header
# and library are hard includes in Mesa's glxcmds.c, so they come first.
-         xf86vidmodeproto   2.3.1    https://www.x.org/releases/individual/proto/xf86vidmodeproto-%VERSION%.tar.gz
-         libXxf86vm         1.1.6    https://www.x.org/releases/individual/lib/libXxf86vm-%VERSION%.tar.xz

# ---- Mesa / llvmpipe ----------------------------------------------------
# Software OpenGL for the whole desktop: WebKit's accelerated compositing,
# GTK's GL paths and the GLX/EGL clients all land on llvmpipe, Mesa's
# LLVM-JIT rasterizer.  llvmpipe needs LLVM built FOR THE TARGET -- the JIT
# runs inside the target process -- which is why the biggest package in the
# whole port sits here.  LLVM only (no clang/lld), X86 backend only, one
# shared libLLVM.  The tarball's inner directory is llvm-project-*.src;
# unpack.sh renames it to llvm-<version> like everything else.
-         llvm               23.1.0   https://github.com/llvm/llvm-project/releases/download/llvmorg-%VERSION%/llvm-project-%VERSION%.src.tar.xz
# Mesa itself.  gallium llvmpipe (+ softpipe as the no-JIT fallback),
# platform x11, GLX (dri path over the software winsys -- system 'likeos'
# has no KMS/DRM, so Mesa selects drisw and renders through XShm), and real
# EGL, which retires the hand-written libEGL stub the port carried while
# there was no GL at all.
-         mesa               25.3.6   https://archive.mesa3d.org/mesa-%VERSION%.tar.xz
# GLU, the classic GL utility library: quadrics, tessellation, projection
# helpers.  mesa-demos requires it, and so does a long tail of older GL
# programs.
-         glu                9.0.3    https://archive.mesa3d.org/glu/glu-%VERSION%.tar.xz
# The classic GL clients -- glxinfo, glxgears, eglinfo and friends -- both as
# the standard proof that GL works at all and as the programs a user expects
# on an X desktop with OpenGL.
-         mesa-demos         9.0.0    https://archive.mesa3d.org/demos/mesa-demos-%VERSION%.tar.xz

# The engine itself.  2.52.6 is the newest stable release that still ships
# the GTK3 API (webkit2gtk-4.1); the GTK4-only builds do not apply here.
-         webkitgtk          2.52.6   https://webkitgtk.org/releases/webkitgtk-%VERSION%.tar.xz

# ---- the applications, part two -----------------------------------------
# Claws Mail, moved from above the WebKit chain: --enable-fancy-plugin makes
# its configure require webkit2gtk-4.1, so it has to follow the engine.
-         claws-mail         4.4.0    https://www.claws-mail.org/releases/claws-mail-%VERSION%.tar.xz

# ---- luakit and its Lua -------------------------------------------------
# Lua 5.1.5: luakit is written against Lua 5.1 (or LuaJIT, which is a far
# larger port for no functional gain here) -- its build looks for lua-5.1 and
# nothing newer, so the last release of the 5.1 line is the right version,
# the same way GTK3 pins the toolkits it needs.  Built twice like GLib and
# ICU: the build host needs the interpreter to run luakit's code generators,
# the target needs the library luakit links.
-         lua                5.1.5    https://www.lua.org/ftp/lua-%VERSION%.tar.gz
# LuaFileSystem: luakit require()s "lfs" at startup for its download and
# session handling.  A tag export (tag v1_9_0); the explicit URL carries the
# upstream tag spelling while the column keeps the dotted version.
-         luafilesystem      1.9.0    https://github.com/lunarmodules/luafilesystem/archive/refs/tags/v1_9_0.tar.gz
# The browser.
-         luakit             2.4.0    https://github.com/luakit/luakit/archive/refs/tags/%VERSION%.tar.gz
